    content:
      fontSize: 18
      color: "#FFFFFF"
      fontFamily: "QuattrocentoSans"
      align: [center, middle]
      wrap: false
      text: |
        <p><strong>2</strong></p>
  - elementId: answer-2-title
    elementType: text
    bounds: [135, 285, 1060, 30]
    content:
      fontSize: 18
      color: "$text"
      fontFamily: "QuattrocentoSans"
      align: [left, middle]
      wrap: false
      text: |
        <p><strong>Does one kernel transfer across heterogeneous graphs?</strong></p>
  - elementId: answer-2-body
    elementType: text
    bounds: [135, 320, 1060, 55]
    content:
      fontSize: 16
      color: "$text"
      fontFamily: "QuattrocentoSans"
      lineHeight: 1.4
      align: [left, top]
      text: |
        <p><strong>Yes.</strong> Same GAT kernel, same harness, same gates on two structurally different graphs. Synthetic controls correctly find no signal on noise in both domains. The negative controls are the evidence.</p>
  - elementId: answer-3-bg
    elementType: shape
    bounds: [60, 405, 1160, 120]
    shapeName: roundRect
    adjustments: [3000]
    fill:
      type: solid
      color: "$lightGray"
  - elementId: answer-3-num
